Problem: Use the Second Derivative Test to find and classify all local extrema of $f(x) = x + \dfrac{4}{x}$ on $(0, \infty)$.

Solution:

Step 1: Find critical numbers

$$\begin{align*}
f'(x) &= 1 - \frac{4}{x^2} = \frac{x^2 - 4}{x^2}
\end{align*}$$

Setting $f'(x) = 0$:
$$\begin{align*}
x^2 - 4 &= 0 \\
x &= 2 \quad \text{(taking positive root since domain is } (0,\infty)\text{)}
\end{align*}$$

Step 2: Find $f''(x)$

$$\begin{align*}
f''(x) &= \frac{d}{dx}\left(1 - \frac{4}{x^2}\right) \\
&= 0 - 4 \cdot \left(-\frac{2}{x^3}\right) \\
&= \frac{8}{x^3}
\end{align*}$$

Step 3: Apply Second Derivative Test

At $x = 2$:
$$\begin{align*}
f''(2) &= \frac{8}{8} = 1 > 0
\end{align*}$$

Since $f''(2) > 0$, $f$ has a local minimum at $x = 2$.

Step 4: Find function value

$$\begin{align*}
f(2) &= 2 + \frac{4}{2} = 2 + 2 = 4
\end{align*}$$

Answer: \boxed{\text{Local minimum at } (2, 4)}

Note: This is also a global minimum on $(0,\infty)$ since $f(x) \to \infty$ as $x \to 0^+$ and as $x \to \infty$.
